WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040
Rosa converts a personally owned nonqualified annuity to fixed lifetime

2
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.900
payments. This is annuity income, not an ordinary partial withdrawal before

3
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:14.280
payments start. Under the applicable General Rule, part of each payment

4
00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.760
can recover her investment and the balance can be taxable.

5
00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:22.240
Suppose the question gives one hundred thousand dollars of adjusted

6
00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:26.560
investment and two hundred thousand dollars of expected return for the

7
00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:31.780
calculation. Dividing investment by expected return gives a

8
00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:33.620
fifty percent exclusion percentage.

9
00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:39.260
Expected return is a tax calculation using the applicable payment terms

10
00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:44.360
and factors; it is not simply a promised investment

11
00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:49.580
profit. Use the supplied facts rather than guessing a

12
00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:53.975
life expectancy. Assume the fixed annual payment is twenty thousand dollars

13
00:00:54.195 --> 00:00:57.203
and the stated exclusion percentage is fifty percent.

14
00:00:57.203 --> 00:01:01.555
Ten thousand dollars recovers investment and ten thousand dollars is taxable

15
00:01:01.555 --> 00:01:05.435
each full year while unrecovered investment remains under these simplified

16
00:01:05.435 --> 00:01:10.375
facts. If the annuity starting date is in twenty twenty-six, total

17
00:01:10.375 --> 00:01:13.995
excluded payments cannot exceed the one hundred thousand dollar investment.

18
00:01:14.675 --> 00:01:18.555
Once that amount has been recovered, later payments are fully taxable

19
00:01:18.555 --> 00:01:19.795
under the stated rule.

20
00:01:20.375 --> 00:01:24.415
Lifetime payments can continue after investment is recovered; the tax

21
00:01:24.415 --> 00:01:27.015
treatment changes, not the lifetime promise.

22
00:01:27.735 --> 00:01:31.235
Variable payments use a different General Rule computation, so do not

23
00:01:31.235 --> 00:01:33.375
apply this fixed-payment ratio to every design.

24
00:01:33.873 --> 00:01:38.633
David dies before his individually owned nonqualified annuity begins income

25
00:01:38.633 --> 00:01:42.853
payments. Assume a beneficiary receives one hundred twenty thousand dollars

26
00:01:42.853 --> 00:01:46.333
in one sum and the contract has eighty thousand dollars of

27
00:01:46.333 --> 00:01:48.893
unrecovered investment, with no other adjustment.

28
00:01:49.673 --> 00:01:52.993
Forty thousand dollars is the earnings portion and generally is taxable

29
00:01:52.993 --> 00:01:58.293
ordinary income. The beneficiary does not exclude the entire payment merely

30
00:01:58.293 --> 00:01:59.793
because it follows a death.

31
00:02:00.433 --> 00:02:05.233
Compare this with qualifying life insurance death proceeds, which follow a

32
00:02:05.233 --> 00:02:10.013
different exclusion rule. The contract's death-benefit formula establishes

33
00:02:10.013 --> 00:02:13.893
the amount payable. Tax law then determines the treatment of that

34
00:02:13.893 --> 00:02:18.673
amount. Those are two separate calculations, even when one insurer issues

35
00:02:18.673 --> 00:02:19.953
both types of products.

36
00:02:20.823 --> 00:02:25.303
For the modern nonqualified contract in this example, an owner's death

37
00:02:25.303 --> 00:02:29.363
before the annuity starting date generally requires distribution of the

38
00:02:29.363 --> 00:02:31.363
entire interest within five years.

39
00:02:32.083 --> 00:02:36.223
A qualifying individual beneficiary can use the statutory life-expectancy

40
00:02:36.223 --> 00:02:40.843
alternative when its requirements are met, including timely commencement

41
00:02:40.843 --> 00:02:42.543
generally within one year.

42
00:02:43.283 --> 00:02:48.283
A surviving spouse designated as beneficiary can have a continuation option

43
00:02:48.283 --> 00:02:52.103
under the law. If death occurs after the annuity starting date,

44
00:02:52.383 --> 00:02:56.083
remaining payments generally must be made at least as rapidly as

45
00:02:56.083 --> 00:03:01.023
the existing method. These rules do not guarantee every payout option

46
00:03:01.023 --> 00:03:06.103
in every contract. They also are not the separate inherited-account rules

47
00:03:06.103 --> 00:03:08.123
for individual retirement arrangements.

48
00:03:10.052 --> 00:03:14.052
Consider a corporation that buys an annuity with company money and

49
00:03:14.052 --> 00:03:15.632
owns all the contract rights.

50
00:03:16.152 --> 00:03:19.112
Naming David as the annuitant does not make him the owner.

51
00:03:20.152 --> 00:03:25.692
Under the general non-natural-owner rule, income on a nonqualified annuity

52
00:03:25.692 --> 00:03:30.352
held by a corporation or other non-natural person can be taxable

53
00:03:30.352 --> 00:03:34.972
currently as ordinary income, rather than receiving the usual

54
00:03:34.972 --> 00:03:39.032
individual-owner deferral. The law has specific exceptions.

55
00:03:39.852 --> 00:03:44.572
These include certain qualified arrangements, a qualifying immediate annuity

56
00:03:45.232 --> 00:03:48.332
and contracts acquired by an estate because of a death.

57
00:03:49.292 --> 00:03:52.352
A trust or entity genuinely holding as an agent for a

58
00:03:52.352 --> 00:03:56.792
natural person requires its actual ownership facts to be examined.

59
00:03:57.692 --> 00:04:00.112
Do not classify every trust the same way.

60
00:04:00.928 --> 00:04:03.688
Here are four questions that organize the analysis.

61
00:04:04.668 --> 00:04:08.748
Is the payment a withdrawal, annuity income or a death distribution?

62
00:04:09.788 --> 00:04:11.668
How much investment remains to be recovered?

63
00:04:12.548 --> 00:04:15.688
Who is the owner, and what tax arrangement holds the contract?

64
00:04:16.608 --> 00:04:20.268
Which timing requirement or exception applies to these facts?

65
00:04:21.128 --> 00:04:24.768
Rosa's fixed income example uses a stated exclusion calculation.

66
00:04:25.528 --> 00:04:30.768
David's beneficiary example separates earnings from unrecovered investment.

67
00:04:31.448 --> 00:04:35.348
The corporation example begins with ownership and the statutory exceptions.

68
00:04:36.228 --> 00:04:39.928
None can be solved by memorizing that all annuities are tax-free,

69
00:04:40.308 --> 00:04:42.288
or that every payment is fully taxable.

70
00:04:43.148 --> 00:04:46.268
The correct answer comes from matching the event and ownership facts

71
00:04:46.268 --> 00:04:47.528
with the applicable rule.


